    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "No compliance statement is supportable. The third of the\nthree outcomes the Directive admits, and the only one a two-valued schema has\nnowhere to put. It is ABSTRACT in practice: every instance carries one of the\nsubclasses below, which record WHY no statement is supportable —\n\n(a) NoThresholdDefined  — the regulation sets no limit for this analyte here;\n(b) MethodInsufficient  — the observation is censored and the method's LOQ\n    exceeds the threshold, so compliance cannot be demonstrated even in\n    principle;\n(c) PreconditionUnmet   — a precondition is unmet: unknown fraction, missing\n    bioavailability covariates, unknown hardness class;\n(d) PossibleExceedance  — the result was measured and assessable, and its\n    interval straddles the limit, so the method cannot decide either way;\n(e) BoundNotEstablished — the record states no bound to compare at all;\n(f) CensoredAmbiguous   — censored with the threshold inside [0, LOD], the\n    narrower case at the DETECTION limit, which no monitoring release states.\n\nCase (b) is the sharpest: a laboratory can report \"not detected\" for a substance\nwhose LOQ is a hundredfold above the legal limit, and a closed-world system\nrecords that as compliance.\n\nPRECEDENCE. A censored result whose LOQ exceeds the threshold has an interval\nthat straddles it too, so (b) and (d) both describe it. Article 3(3b) is\ndecisive — the result \"shall not be considered\" — and such an observation is\nrecorded under MethodInsufficient. This is a rule-layer priority and NOT an\nowl:disjointWith axiom between the two: the same observation may legitimately be\nMethodInsufficient against one jurisdiction's standard and PossibleExceedance\nagainst another's, and class-level disjointness would forbid the two-regulation\nassessment this vocabulary exists to support. Exclusivity lives where it always\nhas, on the comparison properties, per observation-threshold pair.\n\nNOTE ON INFERENCE. This class cannot be defined as \"neither of the other two\",\nbecause under the open-world assumption an unasserted property is not a false\none. It is materialised under a locally closed world; see censo-shapes.ttl."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Indeterminate compliance"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/censo/ComplianceOutcome"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Imidacloprid measured by a method quantifying at 2.065 µg/L against an EU annual-average standard of 0.0068 µg/L: no result the method could return would decide compliance."
      }
    ]
  },
